      "lineNbr": 572,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-06-22T23:50:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This should be the USB_SPI_RX_UNEXPECTED_PACKET error code which is actually missing from the STM32 implementation. That hasn\u0027t been a problem yet because there\u0027s a several of other state validations that will fail if a packet is missed or duplicated but good to know.",
      "revId": "005dcf24384f75464ea7ea93a589113fdf441984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "276640a1_3a0758ca",
        "filename": "chip/g/usb_spi.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 572,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-06-23T02:09:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Changed to return UNEXPECTED_PACKET, but IMO this is a protocol deficiency: it would be good to let the host know what has been received and what was expected.",
      "parentUuid": "dea39a9c_9be60653",
      "revId": "005dcf24384f75464ea7ea93a589113fdf441984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9112e266_d4660e42",
        "filename": "chip/g/usb_spi.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 572,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-06-24T02:25:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s fair. There is a data payload that could be added but no mechanism exists to log the byte array if it had payload data.\n\nFlashrom holds onto the handle for the USB SPI endpoints so nothing else should be interacting with the interface for the duration of the transfer and details about the packets transferred along with the point where the errors are returned are logged by raiden_debug_spi.c and the format allows a combination of EC, USB_SPI, and LibUSB error codes to be mapped to unique values. That combined with information about how much of the transfer has succeeded and how many times it did so is a fairly comprehensive view which allows most USB errors to be distinguished and the status_code is not updated after it has reported a failed value until the transfers have been restarted so we don\u0027t end up with the first error code over written.",
      "parentUuid": "276640a1_3a0758ca",
      "revId": "005dcf24384f75464ea7ea93a589113fdf441984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e4410e37_6a17fef9",
        "filename": "chip/g/usb_spi.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 69,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-06-23T00:34:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not quite following where these sizes are coming from. If the goal is to maximize the alignment of packets to USB transfers then these results should have formulas mapping that matches these formulas:\n\nUSB_SPI_MAX_WRITE_COUNT \u003d USB_SPI_PAYLOAD_SIZE_V2_START + (N * USB_SPI_PAYLOAD_SIZE_V2_CONTINUE) \u003d 58 + N * 60\n\nUSB_SPI_MAX_READ_COUNT \u003d USB_SPI_PAYLOAD_SIZE_V2_RESPONSE + (N * USB_SPI_PAYLOAD_SIZE_V2_CONTINUE) \u003d 60 * N\n\nYou don\u0027t need to calculate the maximum size of the USB_SPI_MAX_WRITE_COUNT for 256 byte arrays, for instance the Servo Micros think that their max USB_SPI_MAX_WRITE_COUNT and USB_SPI_MAX_READ_COUNT counts are equal to 298 for simplicity with the shared buffer. When it\u0027s writing an entire data page it simply does `256 + header` size requests.\n\nThe 5 byte offset should also not be removed. Flashrom has different interfaces depending on the chip that is detected. Those drivers treat the the max_spi_write_count and max_spi_read_count inconsistently with some regarding it as the maximum transfer size and others regarding it as the maximum payload size.",
      "revId": "005dcf24384f75464ea7ea93a589113fdf441984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
